course: biology
lesson: 40
title: Counting animals: legs, arms, wings and tails
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Counting is one of a scientist's best tools: the number of legs, arms or wings tells you what kind of animal you are looking at, even when it looks like something else.
explain: Count a beetle on a leaf: three legs on each side, six in all, and every one of them grows from the middle part of its body, the thorax, one pair on each of its three sections, as in every adult insect.
explain: Spiders have eight legs, octopuses eight arms, squid eight arms plus two longer tentacles, a woodlouse fourteen legs and a crab ten, counting its claws.
explain: Names can trick you: a centipede never has exactly a hundred legs, most millipedes have far fewer than a thousand, and a starfish is not a fish and can have five arms or twenty-four.
explain: Wings tell tales too: bees and wasps have four wings, but a hoverfly dressed up as a wasp has only two, so counting wings unmasks it.
fact: Every adult insect has exactly six legs in three pairs, all fixed to the thorax; a caterpillar has six true legs at the front, and its stumpy back «legs» are fleshy grippers, not true legs.
fact: A spider has eight legs; the two short leg-like feelers by its mouth, the pedipalps, are not legs.
fact: An octopus has eight arms with suckers all along them, and scientists call them arms, not tentacles.
fact: A squid or cuttlefish has eight arms plus two longer tentacles with suckers only on their wide tips, ten limbs in all.
fact: Centipedes have one pair of legs on each body section and always an odd number of pairs, so no centipede has exactly 100 legs; the common brown garden centipede has 30.
fact: Most millipedes have between about 30 and 400 legs, two pairs on each double section; one found in Australia in 2021 had 1,306 legs.
fact: Most starfish have five arms, but some kinds have more, and the sunflower sea star can have up to 24; a lost arm can grow back, and each arm has a small eye at its tip.
fact: All birds have two wings and two legs; most have four toes on each foot, but the ostrich has only two.
fact: A bee has four wings, but in flight the front and back wing on each side hook together, so it looks like two.
fact: True flies, like houseflies, hoverflies and mosquitoes, have only two wings; their back pair has shrunk into tiny knobs called halteres.
fact: A woodlouse has 14 legs and is a crustacean, related to crabs; a crab has ten legs counting its claws, which is why it is called a decapod.
fact: Monkeys have tails, but apes, including gorillas, chimpanzees, orangutans and humans, do not; we keep only a small tailbone.
exercise: How many legs does a beetle have? | Six | Eight | Four | Twelve
exercise: Where are all of an insect's legs fixed? | On the thorax, the middle part of the body | Two on each of the three parts of the body | On the abdomen at the back | Under the head
exercise: How many legs does a spider on the bathroom wall have? | Eight | Six | Ten | Twelve
exercise: A careful counter sees ten leg-like things on a spider. What are the extra two? | Pedipalps, short feelers near the mouth | Two spare legs, used only for climbing walls | Its antennae | Its fangs
exercise: How many arms does an octopus have? | Eight | Ten | Six | Twelve
exercise: How many limbs does a squid have? | Ten: eight arms and two longer tentacles | Eight arms only | Six arms and two tentacles, like an octopus | Twelve arms
exercise: How can you tell a squid's tentacles from its arms? | Tentacles are longer, with suckers only on the wide tips | Tentacles have no suckers at all | Tentacles are shorter and fatter | There is no difference
exercise: Can a centipede have exactly 100 legs? | No, its number of leg pairs is always odd | Yes, every single centipede has exactly 100 legs | Yes, but only the biggest ones | No, they all have exactly 1,000
exercise: How many legs does the common brown garden centipede have? | 30 | 100 | 8 | 1,000
exercise: How many legs do most millipedes have? | Between about 30 and 400 | Exactly 1,000 | Exactly 100 | Six, like insects
exercise: How many arms do most starfish have? | Five | Eight | Three | Seven
exercise: How many toes does a chicken have on each foot? | Four | Two | Three | Five
exercise: How many toes does an ostrich have on each foot? | Two | Four | Three | Only one
exercise: How many wings does a honeybee have? | Four, hooked together in pairs in flight | Two | Six | None, it jumps
exercise: How many wings does a hoverfly have? | Two | Four | Three | Eight
exercise: How many legs does a woodlouse have? | 14 | 6 | 8 | 100
exercise: How many legs does a crab have, counting its claws? | Ten | Eight | Fourteen | Twelve
exercise: Which of these has no tail? | A gorilla | A spider monkey | A capuchin monkey | A baboon
test: got 7 ants in the jar lol how many legs is that | 42 | 49 | 56 | 35
test: 3 spiders in the shed, no wait, 4. how many legs altogether | 32 | 24 | 28 | 36
test: An octopus and a squid share a tank. How many limbs altogether, arms and tentacles? | 18 | 16 | 20 | 14
test: In a cartoon an octopus has ten wiggly tentacles. What would you fix? | It has eight arms; ten limbs is a squid | It has six arms | It has twelve arms | Nothing is wrong
test: A yellow and black insect hovers by the flowers. You count two wings. What is it? | A hoverfly, a harmless fly | A wasp | A honeybee | A bumblebee
test: My mate counted a bee's wings and got two, so bees have two wings, innit? | No, bees have four, hooked together in pairs | Yes, bees have just two wings, like house flies do | Yes, but queens have four | No, bees have six
test: A caterpillar looks like it has 16 legs. How many true legs does it have? | Six, at the front | Sixteen | Eight | None
test: Your cousin says the woodlouse under the stone is an insect. How does counting legs settle it? | It has 14 legs, so it is not an insect but a crustacean | It has 6 legs, so it is an insect | It has 8 legs, so it is a spider | It has no legs, so it is a worm
test: At the farm park you count the toes of two chickens. How many toes in all? | 16 | 8 | 12 | 20
test: In a game, a gorilla swings through the trees by its long tail. What is wrong? | Gorillas are apes and have no tail | Gorillas have short tails only | Nothing, gorillas really do use their tails to swing | Gorillas swing by their ears
test: Is a starfish with six arms a mistake of nature? | No, some kinds have more arms, and a lost arm can regrow | Yes, all starfish must have five | Yes, it is two starfish joined | No, it is a baby octopus
test: A five-armed starfish has how many little eyes? | Five, one at each arm tip | Two, like a fish | None at all | One in the middle
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of legs possessed by a group of five adult insects. | Thirty | Twenty-five | Forty | Fifteen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the limbs of a squid? | Eight arms together with two elongated tentacles | Eight arms and no tentacles | Ten arms, all of equal length and bearing suckers | Six arms and four tentacles
exam: It is asserted that a certain centipede possesses precisely one hundred legs. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The assertion is false, the number of leg pairs being invariably odd | The assertion is true of all centipedes | The assertion is true of garden centipedes only | The assertion is true of the young only
exam: Identify the number of wings borne by a true fly. | Two | Four | Eight | None
